# {{ name | replace(from="-", to=" ") | title }}

A TanStack Start application on Nitro, with signing in already wired.

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:dev      # http://localhost:{{ port }}
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:build
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:preview  # the Nitro server, same port
```

## What is already here

Every route below is wiring. The behaviour lives in `@lilo-moon/auth-tanstack` and the packages
under it, so there is nothing to reimplement and nothing to keep in sync.

| Route                    | What it does                                                |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `/`                      | Signed out. Google and the email-code path, both ways in.   |
| `/api/auth/start`        | Sets the state cookie, hands the browser to the provider.   |
| `/callback`              | Checks state, exchanges the code, creates the organization. |
| `/api/auth/email/start`  | Emails a six digit code.                                    |
| `/api/auth/email/verify` | Checks it, or returns to `/verify-email?retry=true`.        |
| `/verify-email`          | Where the code is typed.                                    |
| `/app`                   | Signed in. Behind the four access states.                   |
| `/session-error`         | A token that verified and made no sense. No sign-in button. |
| `/api/auth/signout`      | Clears the session.                                         |

`src/server/auth.ts` holds the only three decisions an application makes about signing in: the
redirect identity path, where a completed sign-in lands, and where the code is typed.

## Before it can sign anybody in

**Register `http://localhost:{{ port }}/callback` with the AuthKit application, and set
`WORKOS_REDIRECT_URI` to it in `.env.local`.** That variable is a single value shared by every
application in this repo, so two applications cannot both hold a correct one at the same time. Run
one at a time locally, or give each its own WorkOS application.

`DATABASE_URL` is optional. Without it the signed-in page shows the verified claims and no row
counts, which is a runnable state rather than a broken one.

## Where your product goes

`SignedInPanel` takes children. Render your surface there, in
`src/components/signed-in-route.tsx`; the panel puts it under its own heading. `apps/web` passes a
task board. Building blocks live in `@lilo-moon/ui`, composed views in `@lilo-moon/views`, and a
view that more than one application would want belongs in the latter rather than here.

The layout has one seam, and every directory sits on a side of it. `src/routes/` wires, one file
per URL, flat dot notation only. `src/server/` composes what every page shares: the identity
runtime, the services, the access loader. `src/components/` holds shell components shared across
features. Everything a single feature owns, its components and its server logic together, lives in
`src/features/<name>/`, so the files that change together sit together and a dead feature is one
directory to delete. `apps/web` keeps its task board in `src/features/tasks/` as the exemplar.
A test that proves one module takes that module's filename, and `tests/` mirrors the `src/` paths
once it outgrows a flat listing; suite-level tests stay at the `tests/` root. `src/server/services.ts`
is the composition root, one lazy getter per external service and nothing else.
